  "result": "{\"seen_before\": true, \"matches\": [\"WSL#40488\", \"WSL#40616\", \"WSL#40808\"], \"answer\": \"This looks like a known problem. After a WSL 2.7.x install or upgrade, every WSL2 distro fails with Wsl/Service/CreateInstance/CreateVm/HCS/ERROR_FILE_NOT_FOUND. In WSL#40488 the cause was that C:\\\\Program Files\\\\WSL\\\\system.vhd and tools\\\\modules.vhd were missing, so the VM could not attach its disks. No archived ticket mentions 2.7.12.0 specifically, so I can't confirm yours has the same cause. First, check whether those two files exist on your machine. Your downgrade to 2.7.11.0 already fixed it, so staying on that version is a safe workaround. The archived tickets give these fixes. (1) Repair the installer. Download the MSI for your WSL version from the GitHub releases page, then right-click it and choose Repair, or run `msiexec /fa wsl.<version>.x64.msi`. (2) Roll back to an earlier release by installing its MSI (2.6.3 worked for several people in WSL#40488). (3) If the MSI fails silently, disable the Windows Subsystem for Linux feature, reboot, re-enable it, reboot, then reinstall the MSI. According to WSL#40488, the underlying bug was fixed in 2.9.3 and backported to 2.7.14, but the fix only applies when updating from an already-fixed version. If it still fails after a repair, attach logs from collect-wsl-logs.ps1 to a new ticket.\"}",
